## Functional Coverage Limitations — In Simple Words

Functional coverage tells us how much of the design's expected functionality has been tested. But it has one important limitation: it can only measure the features that we have included in the coverage code.

### Let's understand with an example

Suppose you are designing a calculator with 10 features.

- You write functional coverage code for only 7 features.
- You test the calculator and successfully test all 7 features.
- Your functional coverage may show 100% coverage.

But wait! What happened to the other 3 features? They were never included in the coverage code, so they were never measured.

Imagine the calculator has 10 features:

Tested

Tested

Tested

Tested

Tested

Tested

Tested

Missed

Missed

Missed

7 features tested successfully

3 features not included in coverage

The report might show 100%, even though the complete design has not been checked.

### Why is this a limitation?

Because functional coverage does not automatically know every feature in your design. It only tracks what you tell it to track.

If you forget to include a feature in the coverage model, the coverage report will not warn you that the feature is missing.

### How can we avoid this problem?

1. Read the design specification carefully.
2. Identify all the important features and scenarios.
3. Include every relevant feature in the functional coverage code.
4. Compare the coverage model against the design specification to make sure nothing is missed.

### Remember this key point

100% functional coverage does not always mean the entire design has been tested. It means 100% of the features included in the coverage model have been covered.

In simple words: If you don't measure a feature, you won't know whether it has been tested or not.
